<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-domain" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Domain</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.0 Calculus
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.1 Functions
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Define the range and domain of functions
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on activity -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Guidance on the Opening Activity</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    10 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To help learners develop a concrete sense of what "allowed inputs" means before the formal definition is introduced. The activity uses a real-world context (a juice machine) so learners can reason about restrictions without needing any algebraic rules first.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Guide learners to articulate why certain inputs are not allowed rather than simply telling them. Ask questions such as "what happens if you put in a negative number of litres?" or "can the machine work with half a litre?" to draw out the idea of a restricted input. Use their responses to introduce the word domain naturally at the end of the discussion, before moving to the formal definition.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <!-- Add Activity -->

    <activity xml:id="activity-domain-juice-machine">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                A school canteen has a juice machine. The machine produces 200 ml of juice for every fruit placed into it. If <m>x</m> represents the number of fruits, then the amount of juice produced is given by
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>J(x)=200x</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Not every value of <m>x</m> is possible in this situation. Investigate which values make sense and use your findings to determine the domain of the function.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Can the machine accept a negative number of fruits? Explain why or why not.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What happens when <m>x=0</m>? Find <m>J(0)</m> and explain whether the result is reasonable.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose the machine can hold at most 10 fruits at a time. List all possible values of <m>x</m>. Hence, state the domain of the function in this situation.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Now suppose the machine has no upper limit on the number of fruits it can hold. Describe all possible values of <m>x</m> and write the domain using an inequality.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Think of another real-life situation where some input values are not possible. Describe the situation and explain how the restrictions affect the domain.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on structured lesson plan-->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    15 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To formalise the concept of domain that learners have already encountered through the activity, and to introduce the three main types of restrictions they will meet at this level.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways and Teacher Connection</title>
            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> Return to the juice machine context. The set of allowed input values the class described in the activity is exactly what mathematicians call the domain. Use that bridge before writing any algebraic notation on the board.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Three Restrictions to Emphasise:</alert> At Grade 11, learners need to handle three main cases: expressions under a square root must be greater than or equal to zero, denominators must not equal zero, and expressions inside a logarithm must be greater than zero. Keep the instruction focused on these three, and use the rules table in the insight block as a visible reference during worked examples.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Writing the Domain:</alert> Encourage learners to express the domain as an inequality first (for example, <m>x \geq 5</m>) and then describe it in words. Interval notation is introduced as an optional shorthand alongside the inequality, so that learners who find it confusing can always fall back on the inequality form.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>


    <!-- Add insight / key takeaway directly related to activity / learner experience -->

    <insight>
        <p>
            The <term>domain of a function</term> is the collection of all input values that the function can accept without becoming undefined. In other words, the domain tells us which values of <m>x</m> we are allowed to substitute into the function.
        </p>

        <p>
            The table below summarises the three main restrictions you will meet at this level.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table-domain-rules">
            <title>Rules for Finding the Domain</title>
            <tabular halign="left" top="minor">
                <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                    <cell>Function Type</cell>
                    <cell>Restriction</cell>
                    <cell>What to do</cell>
                </row>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Polynomial (e.g. <m>x^2 + 3x</m>)</cell>
                    <cell>None</cell>
                    <cell>All real numbers are allowed</cell>
                </row>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Square root (e.g. <m>\sqrt{x-4}</m>)</cell>
                    <cell>Expression inside the root must be <m>\geq 0</m></cell>
                    <cell>Set the expression <m>\geq 0</m> and solve</cell>
                </row>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Rational (e.g. <m>\dfrac{1}{x-3}</m>)</cell>
                    <cell>Denominator must not equal zero</cell>
                    <cell>Set the denominator equal to zero, solve, then exclude those values</cell>
                </row>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Logarithm (e.g. <m>\ln(x+1)</m>)</cell>
                    <cell>Expression inside the logarithm must be <m>\gt 0</m></cell>
                    <cell>Set the expression <m>\gt 0</m> and solve</cell>
                </row>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Exponential (e.g. <m>5^x</m>)</cell>
                    <cell>None</cell>
                    <cell>All real numbers are allowed</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            The graphs below show two examples. To read the domain from a graph, look at how far the curve stretches left and right along the <m>x</m>-axis.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-domain-graphs">
            <caption>Reading the domain from a graph. The left graph has no restrictions, while the right graph only starts at <m>x = -2</m>.</caption>
            <image xml:id="tikz-domain-graphs" width="90%">
                <shortdescription>
                    Two graphs used to determine domains. The first graph is a parabola with domain all real numbers. The second graph is a square root graph beginning at x equals negative two.
                </shortdescription>
                <latex-image>
                    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]

                        % ================= LEFT GRAPH ===================
                        \begin{scope}[shift={(-5,0)}]
                            \draw[->, thick] (-3.5,0) -- (3.5,0) node[below] {$x$};
                            \draw[->, thick] (0,-1) -- (0,5) node[left] {$y$};
                            \draw[blue, thick, domain=-2.2:2.2, samples=100]
                                plot (\x, {\x*\x});
                            \node at (0,4.5) {\small $y=x^2$};
                            \node[align=center, font=\small] at (0,-2)
                                {Domain: all real numbers\\$x$ can be any value};
                        \end{scope}

                        % ================= RIGHT GRAPH ===================
                        \begin{scope}[shift={(5,0)}]
                            \draw[->, thick] (-4,0) -- (4,0) node[below] {$x$};
                            \draw[->, thick] (0,-1) -- (0,4) node[left] {$y$};
                            \draw[red, thick, domain=-2:4, samples=100]
                                plot (\x, {sqrt(\x+2)});
                            \filldraw[black] (-2,0) circle (2pt);
                            \node at (1.5,3.2) {\small $y=\sqrt{x+2}$};
                            \node[align=center, font=\small] at (0,-2)
                                {Domain: $x \geq -2$\\the graph starts at $x=-2$};
                        \end{scope}

                    \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>
    </insight>

    <exploration>
        <title>Domain of a Function</title>
        <interactive
            xml:id="interactive-domain-explorer"
            platform="jsxgraph"
            width="90%"
            aspect="1:1"
            source="jsxgraph/domain-function.js">

            <slate
                xml:id="jsxgraph-domain-explorer-board"
                surface="jsxboard"
                aspect="1:1"/>
            <!-- <interactive xml:id="interactive-even-odd" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="1:1" source="jsxgraph/even-odd-functions.js">
            <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-even-odd" surface="jsxboard" aspect="1:1"/> -->

            <instructions>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>

                            <p>
                                Click the <alert>New Random Function</alert> button to generate different types of functions.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Observe the <term>green shaded region</term>, which represents the domain of the function that is, all the <m>x</m>-values the function can accept.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Notice how different functions have different restrictions on their domains.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Look carefully at the highlighted boundary points and any vertical dashed lines. These mark where the function starts, stops, or becomes undefined.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Try to write the domain as an inequality before reading the displayed answer.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </instructions>
        </interactive>
    </exploration>

    <!-- Add further instruction (not in a block) to develop ideas as needed -->

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- Add at least 3 examples -->
    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the domain of the polynomial function <m>f(x) = 3x^3 - 2x^2 + 7x - 1</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Polynomial functions do not involve division by a variable, a square root, or a logarithm, so no input value will ever cause the function to be undefined. Every real number can be substituted for <m>x</m> and the function will produce a result.
            </p>
            <p>
                Therefore, the domain is all real numbers, which we can write as <m>x</m> can be any real number.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Determine the domain of the rational function <m>g(x) = \frac{x + 5}{x^2 - 16}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The function is undefined wherever the denominator equals zero, because division by zero is not allowed. We find those values by setting the denominator equal to zero and solving.
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>x^2 - 16 \amp = 0</mrow>
                    <mrow>(x - 4)(x + 4) \amp = 0</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                This gives <m>x = 4</m> or <m>x = -4</m>. These two values must be excluded.
            </p>
            <p>
                The domain is all real numbers except <m>x = 4</m> and <m>x = -4</m>, which we write as <m>x \neq 4</m> and <m>x \neq -4</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Calculate the domain of the square root function <m>h(x) = \sqrt{3x - 15}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The expression inside the square root must be greater than or equal to zero, because the square root of a negative number is not a real number. We solve the inequality to find the allowed inputs.
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>3x - 15 \amp \geq 0</mrow>
                    <mrow>3x \amp \geq 15</mrow>
                    <mrow>x \amp \geq 5</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                The function is only defined when <m>x \geq 5</m>. Therefore, the domain is all real numbers greater than or equal to <m>5</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Determine the domain of the function <m>p(x)</m> represented by the graph below.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-domain-bounded-graph">
                <caption>Graph of a continuous bounded function</caption>
                <image xml:id="tikz-domain-bounded-graph" width="70%">
                    <shortdescription>A curve on a coordinate plane starting at an inclusive solid dot at x = -2 and ending at an exclusive open dot at x = 5.</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
                            % Axes
                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-4,0) -- (7,0) node[below] {$x$};
                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-2) -- (0,4) node[left] {$y$};
                            
                            % Ticks
                            \draw (-2, 0.1) -- (-2, -0.1) node[below left] {$-2$};
                            \draw (5, 0.1) -- (5, -0.1) node[below right] {$5$};
                            
                            % The curve
                            \draw[very thick, blue] (-2,1) .. controls (0,4) and (3,-1) .. (5,2);
                            
                            % Endpoints
                            \filldraw[blue] (-2,1) circle (2.5pt); % Solid dot
                            \filldraw[fill=white, draw=blue, thick] (5,2) circle (2.5pt); % Open dot
                            
                            % Dashed reference lines
                            \draw[dashed, gray] (-2,0) -- (-2,1);
                            \draw[dashed, gray] (5,0) -- (5,2);
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                To find the domain from a graph, we look at how far the curve extends to the left and right along the <m>x</m>-axis.
            </p>
            <p>
                The curve starts at <m>x = -2</m>. The filled dot at this point tells us that <m>x = -2</m> is included in the domain. The curve ends at <m>x = 5</m>, but the open (unfilled) dot there tells us that <m>x = 5</m> is not included.
            </p>
            <p>
                Therefore, the domain is all values of <m>x</m> from <m>-2</m> up to but not including <m>5</m>, which we write as <m>-2 \leq x \lt 5</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the domain of the rational function <m>q(x)</m> plotted in the graph below.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-domain-asymptote-graph">
                <caption>Graph of a rational function with a vertical asymptote</caption>
                <image xml:id="tikz-domain-asymptote-graph" width="70%">
                    <shortdescription>A rational function graphed in two separate pieces, divided by a vertical dashed line indicating an asymptote at x = 1.</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
                            % Axes
                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-4,0) -- (6,0) node[below] {$x$};
                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-3) -- (0,5) node[left] {$y$};
                            
                            % Vertical Asymptote
                            \draw[dashed, very thick, red] (1,-3) -- (1,5) node[above right] {$x=1$};
                            \draw (1, 0.1) -- (1, -0.1) node[below right, text=black] {$1$};
                            
                            % The curves
                            \draw[very thick, purple, domain=-3.5:0.7, samples=50] plot (\x, {1/(\x-1) + 2});
                            \draw[very thick, purple, domain=1.3:5.5, samples=50] plot (\x, {1/(\x-1) + 2});
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The arrows on the left and right ends of the graph show that the function continues in both directions without stopping. However, the red dashed line at <m>x = 1</m> is a vertical asymptote, meaning the curve approaches this line from both sides but never reaches it. The function is undefined at exactly <m>x = 1</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Therefore, the domain is all real numbers except <m>x = 1</m>, which we write as <m>x \neq 1</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- If STACK questions are included, uncomment this: -->

    <!-- <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" /> -->

    <!-- Add STACK exercises if made -->

    <!-- Add static exercises -->

    <exercises xml:id="exercise-domain-function">
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the domain of each of the following functions. Write your answer as an inequality.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">
                        <li>
                            <p><m>f(x)=x^3+2x-1</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>g(x)=\sqrt{x+7}</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>h(x)=\dfrac{1}{x+5}</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>p(x)=\sqrt{3x-9}</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>q(x)=\ln(x-2)</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>r(x)=\dfrac{x+1}{x^2-9}</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>s(x)=5^x</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>t(x)=\sqrt{8-2x}</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>u(x)=\dfrac{3}{x-1}</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>v(x)=\sqrt{x^2-16}</m></p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">
                        <li>
                            <p>All real numbers</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>x \geq -7</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>x \neq -5</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>x \geq 3</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>x \gt 2</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>x \neq -3</m> and <m>x \neq 3</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>All real numbers</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>x \leq 4</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>x \neq 1</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>x \leq -4</m> or <m>x \geq 4</m></p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Calculate the domain of <m>g(x) = \sqrt{x + 9}</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><m>x \geq -9</m></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Find the domain of <m>h(x) = \dfrac{x}{x - 7}</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>All real numbers where <m>x \neq 7</m></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Determine the domain of <m>j(x) = \log_2(5x - 15)</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><m>x \gt 3</m></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Calculate the domain of <m>k(x) = |3x + 4|</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>All real numbers</p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Find the domain of <m>m(x) = \dfrac{x + 2}{x^2 - 36}</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>All real numbers where <m>x \neq -6</m> and <m>x \neq 6</m></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Determine the domain of <m>n(x) = e^{4x} + 2</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>All real numbers</p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Calculate the domain of <m>p(x) = \sqrt{10 - x}</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><m>x \leq 10</m></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Find the domain of <m>q(x) = \tan(x)</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>All real numbers except values where <m>x = (2n + 1)\dfrac{\pi}{2}</m> for any integer <m>n</m>.</p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Determine the domain of <m>r(x) = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2x - 8}}</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><m>x \gt 4</m></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

    </exercises>

    <!-- [LP] Add information on how to differentiate  -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table>
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left">
                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Learner Group</cell>
                    <cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell>
                </row>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Keep the domain rules table from the insight block visible on the board throughout the lesson. Encourage these learners to first identify which type of function they are looking at (polynomial, square root, rational, or logarithm) before attempting any algebra. Focus their practice on polynomial and simple square root questions (parts a, b, d, g in Exercise 1) before moving to rational functions or combined restrictions.
                    </cell>
                </row>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        The core lesson activities as described above.
                    </cell>
                </row>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Ask these learners to find the domain of functions that combine two restrictions at once, such as <m>f(x) = \dfrac{\sqrt{x-1}}{x-4}</m>, where they must apply both the square root rule and the rational function rule together. They can also be asked to sketch a graph for one of the exercise questions and explain how the graph confirms the domain they found algebraically.
                    </cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

